<BaseLayout
  title="Tree Removal Permits in South Florida | What Homeowners Need to Know"
  description="When you need a permit to remove a tree in Miami-Dade, Broward and Palm Beach County, the Florida arborist exemption for dangerous trees, and how to avoid fines."
>
  <PageHero
    badge="PERMITS & REGULATIONS"
    title="Tree Removal Permits in South Florida"
    lead="Before a tree comes down, it pays to know the rules. Here is how permits work in Miami-Dade, Broward and Palm Beach County, and when you may not need one."
  />

  <section class="bg-white py-24">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6">
      <div class="prose-sf">
        <p class="note">
          <strong>Short version:</strong> most South Florida cities require a permit to remove a tree, the rules depend on
          your city and the tree, and dangerous trees on residential property can be exempt with an arborist's written
          assessment. Always confirm with your city before work starts.
        </p>

        <h2>Who sets the rules?</h2>
        <p>
          Tree rules are local. If your property is inside a city, such as Miami, Fort Lauderdale, Hollywood, Pembroke
          Pines or Boca Raton, that city's tree ordinance applies. If you are in an unincorporated area, the county's
          rules apply instead. Many HOAs and condo associations add their own approval on top.
        </p>

        <h2>When a permit is usually required</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Removing a healthy tree above a certain trunk size, often measured at about 4.5 feet above the ground.</li>
          <li>Removing protected or specimen trees, such as large native oaks, or trees in a required landscape buffer.</li>
          <li>Removing trees on commercial property or in common areas.</li>
          <li>Relocating a tree or doing work that changes the required landscaping on a site.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Trimming and pruning usually do not need a permit, but some cities restrict heavy cutting, such as
          "hatracking" a tree, which can count as removal.
        </p>

        <h2>When a permit is often not required</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Many prohibited invasive species, such as Australian pine or Brazilian pepper, in many jurisdictions.</li>
          <li>Dead trees, often with photos or a short inspection.</li>
          <li>Trees that pose a danger on residential property, under the state exemption below.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>The Florida exemption for dangerous trees</h2>
        <p>
          Florida Statute 163.045 limits what a local government can require for trees on residential property. If an
          ISA certified arborist or a Florida licensed landscape architect documents that a tree presents an
          unacceptable risk of failure to people or property, a city or county may not require a permit, fee or
          replacement trees to prune, trim or remove it.
        </p>
        <p>
          Keep the written assessment and photos after the work is done. The exemption covers residential property only,
          and it does not change HOA rules.
        </p>

        <h2>What happens if you skip the permit?</h2>
        <p>
          Cities can issue fines and require replacement trees, sometimes several for each tree removed. The cost of a
          violation is often far higher than the permit itself, so it is worth checking first.
        </p>

        <h2>How we help</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>We tell you what is usually required for your address and type of tree.</li>
          <li>Our <a href="/certified-arborist/">arborist assessments</a> document the tree's condition when a permit or the state exemption needs it.</li>
          <li>We schedule the removal once the paperwork is in order, so the job is done once and done right.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Rules by area</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><a href="/areas/miami-tree-removal/">Miami and Miami-Dade County</a></li>
          <li><a href="/areas/broward-county-tree-removal/">Broward County</a>, including <a href="/areas/fort-lauderdale-tree-removal/">Fort Lauderdale</a>, <a href="/areas/hollywood-tree-service/">Hollywood</a> and <a href="/areas/pembroke-pines-tree-removal/">Pembroke Pines</a></li>
          <li><a href="/areas/palm-beach-county-tree-removal/">Palm Beach County</a>, including <a href="/areas/boca-raton-tree-removal/">Boca Raton</a></li>
        </ul>

        <p class="text-base text-slate-500">
          This page is general information, not legal advice. Ordinances change, so confirm the current rules with your
          city or county before removing a tree.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CtaBand title="Not Sure If You Need a Permit?" text="Tell us where the tree is and what's wrong with it. We'll tell you what's usually required and give you a free estimate." />
